WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.371
Speaker 1: Many of you wrote to me and you
said, David, sir, I'm a big, strong guy, but

00:00:05.371 --> 00:00:11.220
I have tears in my eyes as those stories often
go, because as I looked through this gay marriage

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:19.890
bill that just passed, it's not really clear
that it actually guarantees gay marriage.

00:00:19.890 --> 00:00:24.460
And those people are absolutely correct.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:25.460
Absolutely correct.

00:00:25.460 --> 00:00:26.869
And so I want to talk about that.

00:00:26.869 --> 00:00:32.590
The reporting widespread from over the weekend
and late last week, Bill, to protect same

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:35.550
sex marriage rights clears Congress.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:45.180
The idea here was let's codify gay marriage
before maybe Republicans come for it.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:50.780
And the Supreme Court makes a decision on
gay marriage like they did with Roe v Wade.

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:58.300
We have seen many on the right say the gay
marriage decision was just as wrongly decided

00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:04.510
by a prior Supreme Court, as was Roe v Wade,
making it clear they are setting their sights

00:01:04.510 --> 00:01:08.759
and targeting gay marriage as well as a whole,
but access to contraception and other things

00:01:08.759 --> 00:01:09.970
as well.

00:01:09.970 --> 00:01:14.970
So the idea was let's codify it, let's guarantee
gay marriage.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:19.480
And indeed, the House on Thursday, as The
New York Times writes, gave final approval

00:01:19.480 --> 00:01:26.810
to legislation to mandate federal recognition
for same sex marriages.

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:27.810
Hmm.

00:01:27.810 --> 00:01:29.000
That's interesting.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.460
What does that mean?

00:01:30.460 --> 00:01:35.910
The vote was 258 to 169 with one member voting
present.

00:01:35.910 --> 00:01:40.620
This now goes to Joe Biden, who will, of course,
sign it.

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:44.460
I don't want to sort of like sugarcoat this
or dance around it.

00:01:44.460 --> 00:01:52.530
The legislation doesn't require states to
issue marriage licenses for same sex couples.

00:01:52.530 --> 00:01:55.850
That is not what the legislation does.

00:01:55.850 --> 00:02:02.009
States currently have to do that because of
the 2015 decision from the Supreme Court.

00:02:02.009 --> 00:02:09.340
If that decision were overturned, even despite
this legislation, states would no longer have

00:02:09.340 --> 00:02:12.030
to issue same sex marriages.

00:02:12.030 --> 00:02:22.140
What the law says is that states that that
all states must recognize same sex marriages

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:25.440
that were entered into someplace else.

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:28.780
So let me give you the exact scenario.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:33.580
Imagine now that gay marriage has been codified,
even though it hasn't really.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:40.030
Now that this law has passed, imagine that
in a year or in two years or whenever there

00:02:40.030 --> 00:02:44.370
is a case that gets to the Supreme Court,
much like the case that ultimately overturned

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:48.810
Roe v Wade, gets to the Supreme Court and
the Supreme Court does decide, hey, it was

00:02:48.810 --> 00:02:50.140
wrongly decided.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:51.460
It was wrongly decided.

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:57.440
So we're doing away with the 2015 decision
that made gay marriage that banned bans on

00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.190
same sex marriage.

00:02:59.190 --> 00:03:07.110
Does this new law mean that if Kentucky doesn't
want to anymore, they have to issue same sex

00:03:07.110 --> 00:03:08.110
marriage licenses?

00:03:08.110 --> 00:03:09.110
No, it doesn't.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:16.879
All this law says is that if the 2015 gay
marriage decision is overturned, Kentucky

00:03:16.879 --> 00:03:22.319
would have to recognize same sex marriages
from wherever.

00:03:22.319 --> 00:03:30.000
So you wouldn't be able to deny a gay couple
married elsewhere all of the same rights that

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.900
married people have in Kentucky.

00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:40.330
But it does not mean that Kentucky would have
to issue same sex marriage licenses if the.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:42.739
2015 decision is overturned.

00:03:42.739 --> 00:03:45.280
So it's better than nothing.

00:03:45.280 --> 00:03:51.720
But at the same time, if people don't have
money to travel in order to get married somewhere

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.990
else, it's going to create problems.

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:56.989
It's going to create problems for people.

00:03:56.989 --> 00:04:00.129
It doesn't guarantee gay marriage to anyone.

00:04:00.129 --> 00:04:08.010
It guarantees if you find a place to get married,
it will have to be a recognized marriage in

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.180
all 50 states.

00:04:09.180 --> 00:04:11.150
And again, this is all preemptive.

00:04:11.150 --> 00:04:17.209
It's if the 2015 decision were to be overturned,
that is what it does.

00:04:17.209 --> 00:04:19.019
And that is what it does not do.

00:04:19.019 --> 00:04:20.540
Is it better than nothing?

00:04:20.540 --> 00:04:22.169
Of course it is.

00:04:22.169 --> 00:04:24.360
Is it not really that great?

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:29.580
If the issue is, let's make sure that no matter
what happens, gay and lesbian couples will

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:32.389
continue being able to get married in any
state.

00:04:32.389 --> 00:04:33.720
Yeah, it doesn't do that.

00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:35.169
It absolutely doesn't do that.

00:04:35.169 --> 00:04:37.280
It is what it is important to understand it.

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:40.130
Now we know what it is and we press forward.

